249
00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:44.080
You always rule out delirium before you even

250
00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:45.600
think about assuming dementia.

251
00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:48.960
This is a golden rule because one is a reversible emergency

252
00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:50.800
and the other is a progressive decline.

253
00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:52.080
Delirium is acute.

254
00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:53.360
It comes on fast.

255
00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:56.240
The patient's clarity fluctuates wildly throughout the day.

256
00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:58.400
And crucially, it is medically caused.

257
00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:00.000
Like what kind of medical cause?

258
00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.080
It could be a severe urinary tract infection,

259
00:13:02.080 --> 00:13:05.040
a bad medication interaction, or severe dehydration.

260
00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:07.600
Dementia, on the other hand, is gradual, steady,

261
00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:08.400
and progressive.

262
00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:11.520
So if a clinician mistakes the rapid onset of delirium

263
00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:13.120
for the natural progression of dementia,

264
00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:15.520
they might miss a totally treatable infection,

265
00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:17.920
and the patient could die from a preventable cause.

266
00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:19.760
Which leads us to how clinicians handle

267
00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:22.880
the most acute crisis of all, assessing suicide risk

268
00:13:22.880 --> 00:13:23.400
in the room.

269
00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:26.280
They don't just ask, are you safe, and leave it at that.

270
00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:29.760
No, they use highly structured assessments, most notably

271
00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:34.280
the Columbia Suicide Severity Rating Scale, the CSSRS.

272
00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:36.840
It forces the clinician to evaluate risk

273
00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:39.720
in a specific escalating order.

274
00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:43.880
Ideation, plan, intent, access to lethal means,

275
00:13:43.880 --> 00:13:46.080
and finally, protective factors.

276
00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:47.560
And the sources are incredibly clear

277
00:13:47.560 --> 00:13:50.400
that one specific practice, which used to be standard,

278
00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:53.360
is completely outdated and actually dangerous.

279
00:13:53.360 --> 00:13:56.240
Yes, no suicide contracts.

280
00:13:56.240 --> 00:13:58.680
Having a client in deep distress sign a piece of paper

281
00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:01.240
promising not to hurt themselves is not evidence-based.

282
00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:02.600
It's just paperwork.

283
00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:04.440
It is essentially cover-your-ass paperwork

284
00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:06.760
that might make the clinician feel less anxious,

285
00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:09.440
but does absolutely nothing to protect the client.

286
00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:12.360
The evidence-based mechanism is collaborative safety planning.

287
00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:13.880
Like building a concrete plan of what

288
00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:15.440
to do when things get bad.

289
00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:16.200
Exactly.

290
00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:18.840
You work with a client to build an actionable list of steps.

291
00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:21.920
Who to call, where to go, what coping skills

292
00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:24.200
to use for when the distress peaks

293
00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:26.040
and their cognitive control slips.

294
00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:28.760
So assuming immediate physical safety is secured,

295
00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:31.160
and we know the patient isn't in a life-threatening medical

296
00:14:31.160 --> 00:14:34.560
crisis, the clinician's job shifts.

297
00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:36.480
It becomes much more subtle, because now they

298
00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:39.040
have to figure out the motive behind a behavior.

299
00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:39.600
Right.

300
00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:42.240
Two things can look absolutely identical on the outside,

301
00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:45.960
but they have entirely different internal engines driving them.

302
00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:48.800
This takes us into the personality disorders,

303
00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:51.000
specifically comparing cluster A,

304
00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:53.280
the odd or eccentric personalities,

305
00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:56.280
with cluster C, the anxious or fearful ones.

306
00:14:56.280 --> 00:14:58.920
The best way to understand the power of motive

307
00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:01.720
is to look at our waiting room example from the very beginning.

308
00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:04.720
Comparing schizoid personality disorder from cluster A

309
00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:07.640
with avoidant personality disorder from cluster C,

310
00:15:07.640 --> 00:15:08.840
both are loners.

311
00:15:08.840 --> 00:15:11.200
They avoid social gatherings, they don't have friends,

312
00:15:11.200 --> 00:15:12.680
they spend all their time alone.

313
00:15:12.680 --> 00:15:14.600
We tend to throw a blanket over both of them

314
00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:15.960
and just call them introverts.

315
00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:19.240
Which completely misses the internal reality.

316
00:15:19.240 --> 00:15:22.200
The motive is the ultimate separator here.

317
00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:24.720
The Cosing with Schizoid Personality Disorder

318
00:15:24.720 --> 00:15:27.360
genuinely lacks the drive for connection.

319
00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:28.960
They just prefer solitude.

320
00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:30.000
They prefer it.

321
00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:32.280
They are detached, emotionally flat,

322
00:15:32.280 --> 00:15:34.600
indifferent to praise or criticism,

323
00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:37.200
and simply do not desire close relationships.

324
00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:38.120
They are fine.

325
00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:40.880
But the avoidant person is in agony.

326
00:15:40.880 --> 00:15:41.840
Yes.

327
00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:44.760
The avoidant person desperately wants connection.

328
00:15:44.760 --> 00:15:47.280
They long for intimacy and friendships,

329
00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:49.000
but they are absolutely terrified

330
00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:51.360
of rejection, criticism, and shame.

331
00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:53.280
So their internal engine is fear.

332
00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:54.200
Exactly.

333
00:15:54.200 --> 00:15:56.040
So they isolate to protect themselves

334
00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:58.280
from the pain of potential judgment.

335
00:15:58.280 --> 00:16:00.600
You can never confuse prefers to be alone

336
00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:02.600
with terrified of being around people.

337
00:16:02.600 --> 00:16:04.080
They use a similar kind of nuance

338
00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:06.280
to untangle borderline personality disorder,

339
00:16:06.280 --> 00:16:08.600
bipolar disorder, and complex trauma, don't they?

340
00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:11.800
Because all three involve massive destructive mood swings.

341
00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:13.240
This is a diagnostic knot

342
00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:15.400
that trips up even seasoned professionals.

343
00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:17.920
To separate them, you look at the tempo and the trigger.

344
00:16:17.920 --> 00:16:20.000
Borderline mood shifts are rapid changing

345
00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:21.360
in minutes or hours,

346
00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:23.160
and they are almost entirely reactive.

347
00:16:23.160 --> 00:16:24.280
Reactive to what?

348
00:16:24.280 --> 00:16:25.640
They are interpersonally triggered

349
00:16:25.640 --> 00:16:27.320
by a perceived rejection,

350
00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:29.360
abandonment, or relationship rupture.

351
00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:30.640
Whereas bipolar.

352
00:16:30.640 --> 00:16:33.000
Bipolar episodes are sustained.

353
00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:35.480
The highs and lows last for days or weeks,

354
00:16:35.480 --> 00:16:36.920
and they aren't necessarily triggered

355
00:16:36.920 --> 00:16:38.520
by an argument with a partner.

356
00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:40.120
They rise and fall on their own

357
00:16:40.120 --> 00:16:41.960
biological episodic rhythm.

358
00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:43.080
And complex trauma.

359
00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:45.200
That emotional dysregulation sits on top

360
00:16:45.200 --> 00:16:47.960
of a chronic long-term trauma history,

361
00:16:47.960 --> 00:16:50.240
often presenting with severe nervous system,

362
00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:52.320
hyperarousal, and dissociation.

363
00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:54.520
Okay, here's where it gets really interesting.

364
00:16:54.520 --> 00:16:56.120
Moving into obsessive behaviors,

365
00:16:56.120 --> 00:16:58.480
there is a fascinating mechanical difference

366
00:16:58.480 --> 00:17:00.800
between obsessive-compulsive disorder,

367
00:17:00.800 --> 00:17:02.600
the anxiety condition,

368
00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:05.560
and obsessive-compulsive personality disorder, OCPD.

369
00:17:05.560 --> 00:17:06.400
Oh, yeah.

370
00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:08.560
Let's say my desk is obsessively organized,

371
00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:10.440
all my pens are perfectly parallel,

372
00:17:10.440 --> 00:17:11.880
I color-code my inbox,

373
00:17:11.880 --> 00:17:14.440
and I get deeply enboyed if someone moves my stapler.

374
00:17:14.440 --> 00:17:15.760
Is that OCD?

375
00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:16.600
No.

376
00:17:16.600 --> 00:17:17.440
And the reason comes down

377
00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:20.080
to two foundational psychological concepts.

378
00:17:20.080 --> 00:17:22.360
Egotysonic versus egosyntonic.

379
00:17:22.360 --> 00:17:24.920
OCD, the anxiety disorder, is egotysonic.

380
00:17:24.920 --> 00:17:26.040
Meaning what, exactly?

381
00:17:26.040 --> 00:17:29.080
It features highly intrusive, unwanted thoughts,

382
00:17:29.080 --> 00:17:31.040
and the person performs rituals

383
00:17:31.040 --> 00:17:34.440
to neutralize the severe anxiety those thoughts cause.

384
00:17:34.440 --> 00:17:36.000
The person hates the thoughts.

385
00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:38.800
They don't wanna be flicking the light switch 20 times.

386
00:17:38.800 --> 00:17:40.560
It causes them immense distress.

387
00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:41.400
And the treatment for that

388
00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:44.720
is exposure and response prevention, or ERP, right?

389
00:17:44.720 --> 00:17:46.760
Where you essentially force the brain

390
00:17:46.760 --> 00:17:48.240
to sit in the anxiety.

391
00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:49.080
Exactly.

392
00:17:49.080 --> 00:17:52.080
In ERP, you expose the person to the trigger, say,

393
00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:53.400
a disorganized desk,

394
00:17:53.400 --> 00:17:56.240
and prevent them from doing the organizing compulsion.

395
00:17:56.240 --> 00:17:58.280
They sit in the fire of their own anxiety

396
00:17:58.280 --> 00:18:00.640
until their brain naturally habituates and learns

397
00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:03.280
that no actual catastrophe is going to happen.

398
00:18:03.280 --> 00:18:05.120
But OCPD is completely different

399
00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:07.400
because OCPD is egosyntonic.

400
00:18:07.400 --> 00:18:08.440
Yes.

401
00:18:08.440 --> 00:18:10.840
The person loves their rigid perfectionism.

402
00:18:10.840 --> 00:18:13.000
They don't have true intrusive obsessions

403
00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:14.840
or compulsions driven by fear.

404
00:18:14.840 --> 00:18:16.920
They just believe their highly controlled,

405
00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:18.280
orderly way of doing things

406
00:18:18.280 --> 00:18:19.960
is the absolute correct way to live,

407
00:18:19.960 --> 00:18:21.960
and everyone else is sloppy and wrong.

408
00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:23.880
So you can't really treat that with ERP.

409
00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:25.920
You can't use ERP on someone with OCPD

410
00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:28.880
because there is no underlying fear to obituate them too.

411
00:18:28.880 --> 00:18:29.880
They think they're right.

412
00:18:29.880 --> 00:18:33.360
The motive completely flips the diagnosis.

413
00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:36.000
The guidelines also explore this internal

414
00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:39.040
versus external motive with deliberate deception,

415
00:18:39.040 --> 00:18:40.480
like faking being sick.

416
00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:41.320
Right.

417
00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:43.160
If someone is intentionally faking an illness

418
00:18:43.160 --> 00:18:45.160
or even physically injuring themselves

419
00:18:45.160 --> 00:18:47.160
just to occupy the sick role,

420
00:18:47.160 --> 00:18:49.120
to get the care, sympathy, and attention

421
00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:50.360
of being a patient,

422
00:18:50.360 --> 00:18:54.000
with no other reward that is fictitious disorder.

423
00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:56.040
The motive is purely internal.

424
00:18:56.040 --> 00:18:56.880
Yes.

425
00:18:56.880 --> 00:18:57.840
But if they are faking a back injury

426
00:18:57.840 --> 00:18:59.880
to get a massive worker's compensation payout

427
00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:02.920
or pretending to be schizophrenic to dodge a criminal trial.

428
00:19:02.920 --> 00:19:04.640
That is malingering.

429
00:19:04.640 --> 00:19:07.440
The deception is driven purely by external gain.

430
00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:10.120
And crucially, malingering isn't even classified

431
00:19:10.120 --> 00:19:12.160
as a mental disorder in the DSM.

432
00:19:12.160 --> 00:19:13.760
It's simply a behavior.

433
00:19:13.760 --> 00:19:15.960
The motive is the master key.

434
00:19:15.960 --> 00:19:18.120
The sources touch on behavioral addictions

435
00:19:18.120 --> 00:19:19.400
and sexuality as well,

436
00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:21.320
and the distress motive applies there too.

437
00:19:21.320 --> 00:19:23.120
I was actually surprised to learn that gambling

438
00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:25.280
is the only recognized behavioral addiction

439
00:19:25.280 --> 00:19:27.120
in the current diagnostic manual.

440
00:19:27.120 --> 00:19:29.680
It sits right next to substance use disorders

441
00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:31.800
because, neurobiologically,

442
00:19:31.800 --> 00:19:35.520
it hijacks the exact same dopamine reward circuitry

443
00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:37.560
in the brain as cocaine or heroin.

444
00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:38.400
Wow.

445
00:19:38.400 --> 00:19:40.720
And because it's a cognitive and behavioral loop,

446
00:19:40.720 --> 00:19:43.240
you treat it with cognitive behavioral therapy.

447
00:19:43.240 --> 00:19:45.160
CBT doesn't use a pill.

448
00:19:45.160 --> 00:19:48.360
It works by identifying the cognitive distortions

449
00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:50.040
like the gambler's fallacy,

450
00:19:50.040 --> 00:19:52.480
the belief that a win is mathematically due

451
00:19:52.480 --> 00:19:54.240
after a losing streak,

452
00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:57.560
and systematically dismantling those faulty thought patterns.

453
00:19:57.560 --> 00:19:59.720
When the sources discuss gender dysphoria

454
00:19:59.720 --> 00:20:01.320
and paraphilic disorders,

455
00:20:01.320 --> 00:20:03.640
they are incredibly careful with how they define

456
00:20:03.640 --> 00:20:05.560
what is and isn't pathological.

457
00:20:05.560 --> 00:20:07.640
The language is precise for a reason.

458
00:20:07.640 --> 00:20:10.920
The critical takeaway is that being transgender

459
00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:13.120
or having an atypical sexual interest

460
00:20:13.120 --> 00:20:14.680
is not a mental disorder.

461
00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:17.560
The identity or the interest itself is not pathology.

462
00:20:17.560 --> 00:20:18.520
Exactly.

463
00:20:18.520 --> 00:20:20.040
The disorder is strictly defined

464
00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:22.800
by the presence of clinically significant distress,

465
00:20:22.800 --> 00:20:26.120
the dysphoria itself, or, in the case of paraphilias,

466
00:20:26.120 --> 00:20:29.160
if the behavior involves harm to non-consenting others.

467
00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:31.680
Distress and harm are the clinical markers.

468
00:20:31.680 --> 00:20:33.560
So understanding all these complex engines,

469
00:20:33.560 --> 00:20:35.220
all these diagnostic rules and master keys,

470
00:20:35.220 --> 00:20:36.800
brings up a massive question.

471
00:20:36.800 --> 00:20:38.760
How do clinicians ethically wield

472
00:20:38.760 --> 00:20:41.800
this kind of analytical power over another human being?

473
00:20:41.800 --> 00:20:43.920
This is the clinician's compass.

474
00:20:43.920 --> 00:20:45.960
The ethical guardrails are rigid.

475
00:20:45.960 --> 00:20:48.000
Client welfare always comes first.

476
00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:50.440
Confidentiality is the absolute default.

477
00:20:50.440 --> 00:20:52.120
And when you are in a gray area,

478
00:20:52.120 --> 00:20:53.920
you consult your clinical supervisor

479
00:20:53.920 --> 00:20:56.360
and you document every single decision.

480
00:20:56.360 --> 00:20:59.040
But confidentiality is an absolute trait.

481
00:20:59.040 --> 00:21:00.000
No.

482
00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:02.120
The most famous exception is the pterosoph

483
00:21:02.120 --> 00:21:04.580
ruling the duty to warn and protect.

484
00:21:04.580 --> 00:21:07.220
If a client presents a specific imminent threat

485
00:21:07.220 --> 00:21:08.820
to an identifiable victim,

486
00:21:08.820 --> 00:21:10.680
the duty to protect public safety

487
00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:13.000
overrides the client's confidentiality.

488
00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:14.640
The therapist must warn the victim

489
00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:16.200
and notify law enforcement.

490
00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:18.680
Then there is mandated reporting for abuse.

491
00:21:18.680 --> 00:21:21.640
Clinicians are legally mandated reporters.

492
00:21:21.640 --> 00:21:23.560
If there is a reasonable suspicion of abuse

493
00:21:23.560 --> 00:21:25.420
or neglect of a protected class,

494
00:21:25.420 --> 00:21:28.520
a child, an elder, or a dependent adult,

495
00:21:28.520 --> 00:21:30.320
they must report it to the state authorities.

496
00:21:30.320 --> 00:21:31.320
Wait, let me stop you there

497
00:21:31.320 --> 00:21:33.100
because the phrase reasonable suspicion

498
00:21:33.100 --> 00:21:34.640
feels incredibly broad.

499
00:21:34.640 --> 00:21:36.040
But don't you need proof?

500
00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:37.800
It seems wild to report someone

501
00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:40.080
and involve the state just based on a suspicion

502
00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:41.960
without investigating first.

503
00:21:41.960 --> 00:21:44.600
You could destroy family over a misunderstanding.

504
00:21:44.600 --> 00:21:46.720
It feels entirely counterintuitive,

505
00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:48.200
especially for a profession

506
00:21:48.200 --> 00:21:50.280
built on gathering information.

507
00:21:50.280 --> 00:21:52.760
But the systemic logic is built entirely around

508
00:21:52.760 --> 00:21:55.420
minimizing risk to the vulnerable.

509
00:21:55.420 --> 00:21:57.940
The clinical guidelines are explicit.

510
00:21:57.940 --> 00:22:00.960
The therapist is a tripwire, not a detective.

511
00:22:00.960 --> 00:22:01.860
A tripwire.

512
00:22:01.860 --> 00:22:04.880
Investigating is the state agency's job.

513
00:22:04.880 --> 00:22:08.280
If a therapist delays reporting to play detective, say,

514
00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:09.880
they decide to interview the parents

515
00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:11.800
to verify a child's story,

516
00:22:11.800 --> 00:22:14.160
they might inadvertently tip off an abuser,

517
00:22:14.160 --> 00:22:16.000
giving them time to coach the child,

518
00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:18.800
destroy evidence, or further harm the victim.

519
00:22:18.800 --> 00:22:21.600
You do not delay protection to gain absolute certainty.

520
00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:22.840
You report the suspicion,

521
00:22:22.840 --> 00:22:25.400
and you let the trained investigators do their job.

522
00:22:25.400 --> 00:22:26.320
That makes total sense

523
00:22:26.320 --> 00:22:27.640
when you frame it as being a tripwire.

524
00:22:27.640 --> 00:22:30.560
You stay in your lane to protect the system's integrity.

525
00:22:30.560 --> 00:22:32.220
And to document all of this,

526
00:22:32.220 --> 00:22:33.600
to prove what they saw in the room,

527
00:22:33.600 --> 00:22:36.720
clinicians use the mental status exam, the MSE.

528
00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:40.160
Because they hold such immense ethical and legal power,

529
00:22:40.160 --> 00:22:42.760
they need a hyper-objective, standardized way

530
00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:45.120
to record their observations.

531
00:22:45.120 --> 00:22:48.080
The MSE is the snapshot of now.

532
00:22:48.080 --> 00:22:49.760
It's not the client's trauma history.

533
00:22:49.760 --> 00:22:51.600
It's a highly structured observation

534
00:22:51.600 --> 00:22:54.600
of how the client looks, sounds, and thinks

535
00:22:54.600 --> 00:22:56.680
in that exact current moment.

536
00:22:56.680 --> 00:22:59.640
The sources highlight two massive clinical distinctions

537
00:22:59.640 --> 00:23:01.820
clinicians have to make during this exam.

538
00:23:01.820 --> 00:23:03.960
The first is mood versus effect.

539
00:23:03.960 --> 00:23:07.720
Think of mood as the climate and effect as the weather.

540
00:23:07.720 --> 00:23:09.480
Mood is what the patient reports feeling.

541
00:23:09.480 --> 00:23:10.840
You quote them in the chart.

542
00:23:10.840 --> 00:23:12.840
The patient states they feel devastated.

543
00:23:12.840 --> 00:23:14.280
And affect is the weather.

544
00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:16.200
Right, so what the clinician objectively observes

545
00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:18.080
on the patient's face, in their vocal tone,

546
00:23:18.080 --> 00:23:19.680
and in their body language.

547
00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:21.280
The crucial diagnostic clue

548
00:23:21.280 --> 00:23:23.320
is when the two are incongruent.

549
00:23:23.320 --> 00:23:25.400
A patient might report feeling completely hopeless

550
00:23:25.400 --> 00:23:28.680
and actively suicidal, but their affect is bright,

551
00:23:28.680 --> 00:23:31.080
they're smiling, and they're laughing at jokes.

552
00:23:31.080 --> 00:23:33.240
That mismatch is a massive red flag.

553
00:23:33.240 --> 00:23:35.680
Exactly, it shows that something more complex is happening.

554
00:23:35.680 --> 00:23:36.840
Climate versus weather.

555
00:23:36.840 --> 00:23:38.880
And the second distinction is thought process

556
00:23:38.880 --> 00:23:40.340
versus thought content.

557
00:23:40.340 --> 00:23:42.020
Thought process is the form.

558
00:23:42.020 --> 00:23:43.700
Content is the material.

559
00:23:43.700 --> 00:23:46.420
Thought process is the shape and flow of their thinking.

560
00:23:46.420 --> 00:23:48.260
Is it logical and linear?

561
00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:50.260
Or is it racing, disorganized,

562
00:23:50.260 --> 00:23:52.180
and jumping wildly from topic to topic?

563
00:23:52.180 --> 00:23:53.220
And thought content.

564
00:23:53.220 --> 00:23:55.540
That's the actual substance of their ideas.

565
00:23:55.540 --> 00:23:57.300
Are they thinking about self-harm?

566
00:23:57.300 --> 00:23:58.920
Do they have a fixed delusion

567
00:23:58.920 --> 00:24:00.580
that the CIA is tracking them?

568
00:24:00.580 --> 00:24:03.420
Gathering all this data ultimately leads a counselor

569
00:24:03.420 --> 00:24:05.420
to a final choice.

570
00:24:05.420 --> 00:24:07.420
Can I effectively treat this person

571
00:24:07.420 --> 00:24:09.260
or do I need to refer them out?

572
00:24:09.260 --> 00:24:11.100
Recognizing the biological limits

573
00:24:11.100 --> 00:24:12.900
of talk therapy is critical.

574
00:24:12.900 --> 00:24:15.620
Talk therapy can rewire cognitive patterns,

575
00:24:15.620 --> 00:24:19.080
but it cannot fix a purely biological crisis.

576
00:24:19.080 --> 00:24:21.180
If a client presents with narcolepsy,

577
00:24:21.180 --> 00:24:24.700
severe obstructive sleep apnea causing cognitive decline,

578
00:24:24.700 --> 00:24:27.580
severe ADHD that requires stimulant medication,

579
00:24:27.580 --> 00:24:30.180
or is going through a dangerous alcohol withdrawal,

580
00:24:30.180 --> 00:24:31.640
the clinician has a duty to refer them

581
00:24:31.640 --> 00:24:34.120
to a medical prescriber or a specialist.

582
00:24:34.120 --> 00:24:36.060
You operate within your scope of practice

583
00:24:36.060 --> 00:24:37.400
and you coordinate care.

584
00:24:37.400 --> 00:24:40.040
It is an incredible balancing act.

585
00:24:40.040 --> 00:24:41.560
We started by looking at this messy,

586
00:24:41.560 --> 00:24:43.240
tangled web of human behavior,

587
00:24:43.240 --> 00:24:45.640
and what we found is that diagnosing the human mind

588
00:24:45.640 --> 00:24:48.420
requires a profound synthesis of skills.

589
00:24:48.420 --> 00:24:50.680
You have to hold strict neurobiological timelines

590
00:24:50.680 --> 00:24:53.320
in one hand, deep human empathy in the other,

591
00:24:53.320 --> 00:24:56.920
and wrap the entire process in rigid ethical boundaries.

592
00:24:56.920 --> 00:24:58.440
And the truly remarkable part

593
00:24:58.440 --> 00:25:01.160
is that clinicians do all of this in real time.

594
00:25:01.160 --> 00:25:03.820
Sitting across from someone in profound distress,

595
00:25:03.820 --> 00:25:05.760
constantly analyzing whether the engine

596
00:25:05.800 --> 00:25:09.040
driving the behavior is fear, chemical imbalance,

597
00:25:09.040 --> 00:25:11.080
or an internal desire for connection.

598
00:25:11.080 --> 00:25:14.080
Which leaves me with a final thought for you to mull over.

599
00:25:14.080 --> 00:25:16.500
We just burped down how the mental status exam

600
00:25:16.500 --> 00:25:19.520
relies entirely on a human being observing the effect

601
00:25:19.520 --> 00:25:21.280
the real time, subtle weather

602
00:25:21.280 --> 00:25:23.660
of another human being in the present moment.

603
00:25:23.660 --> 00:25:26.080
With AI and algorithms rapidly evolving,

604
00:25:26.080 --> 00:25:28.560
constantly trying to track our biometric data,

605
00:25:28.560 --> 00:25:30.700
analyze the micro tremors in our voices

606
00:25:30.700 --> 00:25:32.000
and predict our moods,

607
00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:35.560
could a machine ever truly perform a mental status exam?

608
00:25:35.600 --> 00:25:37.820
Or does spotting the quiet, chilling mismatch

609
00:25:37.820 --> 00:25:40.480
between a heartbreaking story and incongruent smile

610
00:25:40.480 --> 00:25:42.840
require the very human intuition and empathy

611
00:25:42.840 --> 00:25:44.280
we've been talking about today?

612
00:25:44.280 --> 00:25:45.280
Think on that.

613
00:25:45.280 --> 00:25:46.880
Thanks for joining us on this deep dive

614
00:25:46.880 --> 00:25:49.300
and as always, keep feeding your curiosity.
